    "question": {
      ":question/id": "435d0a46-64c5-5b16-be41-70a25c953844",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $t^2-9t+\\frac{81}{4}$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$t^2-9t+\\frac{81}{4}=(t-$ {{field-1}} $)^2+$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{9}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^2-9t+\\frac{81}{4}&=\\underbrace{t^2-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}t+\\frac{81}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(t-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(t-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-9t$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{9}{2}$ and $0$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
